[SPEAKER_13]: Mayor Lungo-Kern?

39
00:02:13,211 --> 00:02:13,991
[SPEAKER_05]: Present, seven.

40
00:02:14,792 --> 00:02:16,273
[SPEAKER_05]: Present, zero, absent.

41
00:02:21,413 --> 00:02:21,613
[SPEAKER_05]: Ms.

42
00:02:21,933 --> 00:02:24,154
[SPEAKER_05]: DeMurphy, Dr. Marisa Edwards-Vinson.

43
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:29,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Good afternoon or good evening, everyone.

44
00:02:31,118 --> 00:02:34,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Today's going to be our kickoff for our official budget process.

45
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:44,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And we have a full agenda, a lot of information to share with the committee this evening.

46
00:02:46,995 --> 00:02:49,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Mister Murphy has spent a significant amount of time.

47
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:57,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Cataloging and organizing this data for tonight's presentation for the different departments.

48
00:02:57,628 --> 00:03:01,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So we have a full schedule ahead of us so.

49
00:03:02,074 --> 00:03:13,765
[SPEAKER_01]: I look forward to tonight's presentation and passing the baton to Mr. Murphy so that he can take us through the slides and let us discuss the departments that are here today.

50
00:03:13,785 --> 00:03:22,713
[SPEAKER_01]: And I thank our staff members that are on the call as well, who will have an opportunity to weigh in if necessary at particular points.

51
00:03:23,374 --> 00:03:24,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So thank you.

52
00:03:24,315 --> 00:03:27,598
[SPEAKER_01]: And Mr. Murphy, please take over.

53
00:03:29,694 --> 00:03:33,037
[SPEAKER_07]: First of all, is everyone able to hear me?

54
00:03:33,077 --> 00:03:35,719
[SPEAKER_07]: Can we do a strophal thumbs up?

55
00:03:35,819 --> 00:03:36,179
[SPEAKER_07]: Thank you.

56
00:03:36,239 --> 00:03:36,540
[SPEAKER_07]: Great.

57
00:03:38,261 --> 00:03:44,867
[SPEAKER_07]: And Dr. Cushing, I just shared with you a document that I could also share with Ms.

58
00:03:44,907 --> 00:03:56,737
[SPEAKER_07]: Galussi as a backup plan, because I like it sounds like a few of you are having a few technical problems this evening that I don't

59
00:03:57,421 --> 00:03:59,362
[SPEAKER_07]: expect they're going to be solved in the next few seconds.

60
00:03:59,443 --> 00:04:07,008
[SPEAKER_07]: And so the deck that we're going to walk through tonight, if either Dr. Cushing or Ms.

61
00:04:07,028 --> 00:04:14,113
[SPEAKER_07]: Galussi are able to share that, I just got the microphone and camera on one of my computers working.

62
00:04:14,153 --> 00:04:22,219
[SPEAKER_07]: And so I don't think I'm going to continue to play with this to get to the point where I can also share my screen.

63
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:23,200
[SPEAKER_07]: That would seem too ambitious.

64
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,661
[SPEAKER_07]: I see Dr. Cushing's in the document.

65
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:26,443
[SPEAKER_07]: So we should be good to go.

66
00:04:27,903 --> 00:04:37,411
[SPEAKER_07]: Peter, if I could just do a few more seconds of introduction and then we'll start going through the various slides that we have for you tonight.

67
00:04:37,471 --> 00:04:49,302
[SPEAKER_07]: So as the superintendent said, tonight is our first formal budget meeting specific to the development of the FY23 budget.

68
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:52,045
[SPEAKER_07]: As the school committee is aware,

69
00:04:52,942 --> 00:05:15,508
[SPEAKER_07]: We have been updating you first quarterly throughout the year, and then over the course of the last month or two, we've been discussing in greater detail both the budgetary priorities for the Medford Public Schools coming into FY23, as well as the budgetary challenges that we know we're going to face coming into this fiscal year.

70
00:05:15,528 --> 00:05:21,289
[SPEAKER_07]: A lot of them, frankly, are similar to budgetary challenges that we would face in any fiscal year, and others are

71
00:05:22,173 --> 00:05:23,975
[SPEAKER_07]: very specific to FY23.

72
00:05:24,976 --> 00:05:49,417
[SPEAKER_07]: As we discussed at your meeting on May 2nd, given that the district is currently in the midst of collective bargaining negotiations with nine of our 10 unions, and given that the vast majority of our operating budget is consumed by personnel costs, as is the case for all school districts,

73
00:05:50,417 --> 00:06:01,141
[SPEAKER_07]: There is a, that is a significant variable and there's a significant degree of uncertainty that we as an organization have coming into this fiscal year.

74
00:06:02,321 --> 00:06:17,727
[SPEAKER_07]: That being said, we have a responsibility both to continue to pursue our educational and operational objectives in the upcoming fiscal year

75
00:06:18,303 --> 00:06:41,376
[SPEAKER_07]: And so we'll talk a little bit tonight about how those two things will interact with one another and how we'll try to adhere to our commitment to the community with respect to transparency and how we'll continue to put student interests and student outcomes at the center of all of our strategic priorities, including those that have a monetary impact.

76
00:06:42,157 --> 00:06:47,060
[SPEAKER_07]: I will now, as we start to go through the slides, and I think we can go now, Peter, if you're ready,

77
00:06:49,624 --> 00:07:01,852
[SPEAKER_07]: Whenever we talk about the budget, well, I know for some committee members, and particularly for some of you that have been on the committee for some time, there can be some degree of repetition and redundancy as you hear me walk through the various steps and sequences.

78
00:07:01,912 --> 00:07:09,737
[SPEAKER_07]: But I think it was Miss McLaughlin who pointed out, as we were sort of mapping out our FY23 budgetary timeline and process,

79
00:07:10,497 --> 00:07:19,740
[SPEAKER_07]: that the budget really is an opportunity for members of the community to have an opportunity to see how the organization functions, to hear about what priorities we're pursuing.

80
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:28,783
[SPEAKER_07]: And so given that, I try to sort of provide a little bit of foundation knowledge at the beginning of the budgetary process, just in terms of how school districts are funded.

81
00:07:29,183 --> 00:07:39,467
[SPEAKER_07]: I'll go through those as quickly as I can, because again, we have been discussing this on almost a weekly basis over the past month or two, but at the same time, I do think it's important context.

82
00:07:40,654 --> 00:07:49,207
[SPEAKER_07]: And then in our first substantive slide, you'll see a slightly modified version of a document that you saw last year that walks through the various steps.

83
00:07:49,448 --> 00:07:52,192
[SPEAKER_07]: And that first step with regard to the strategic plan

84
00:07:52,728 --> 00:07:58,632
[SPEAKER_07]: is one that I think in any budget process, given how much we have going on, it can be easy to skip over.

85
00:07:58,953 --> 00:08:04,777
[SPEAKER_07]: But it's important that we start with our goals and what the objectives are that we're looking to pursue.

86
00:08:04,797 --> 00:08:19,327
[SPEAKER_07]: The members of the school committee, as well as members of the school administration, filled out budget surveys in which essentially you had the opportunity to thinking in the context of the strategic plan, what are the issues and priorities that you believe the district needs to pursue.

87
00:08:20,628 --> 00:08:23,549
[SPEAKER_07]: at that third step with regard to the budget meetings, that's where we are right now.

88
00:08:23,589 --> 00:08:29,371
[SPEAKER_07]: This is our opportunity to, in public, flesh out those priorities and to talk a little bit about how they interact with one another.

89
00:08:30,131 --> 00:08:45,977
[SPEAKER_07]: And really, one of the things that we'll talk about tonight is how we're in a very interesting budgetary time in which we have all the uncertainty and speculation that often comes in Massachusetts with school districts without knowing what the availability of funding is going to be.

90
00:08:46,357 --> 00:08:47,117
[SPEAKER_07]: And at the same time,

91
00:08:47,587 --> 00:08:57,936
[SPEAKER_07]: we've been fortunate enough, both on the municipal side and on the school side, through the ARPA and ESSER funding, to have a significant amount of one-time funding resources available.

92
00:08:58,376 --> 00:09:15,290
[SPEAKER_07]: But some of the strategic considerations that we in the school administration have to take into account, and that you as the school committee have to take into account, is how do we balance those one-time funding priorities against the reoccurring priorities, and how do we determine, essentially, which budget those priorities land on?

93
00:09:15,630 --> 00:09:40,821
[SPEAKER_07]: And we'll talk a little bit tonight about what the implications of those decisions are, what the risks are for potential deficits that we can't fund in the future, but then also what are the risks for immediate student needs that we know exist right now in the somewhat aftermath, or at least hopefully the later stages of the pandemic and the disruptions that it causes.

94
00:09:41,342 --> 00:09:44,303
[SPEAKER_07]: These meetings lead up to the superintendent's recommendation

95
00:09:44,739 --> 00:09:46,820
[SPEAKER_07]: I think oftentimes there's some confusion about that.

96
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:55,663
[SPEAKER_07]: There's an assumption that the municipal government sends over a number and the superintendent then puts it in an envelope and hands it over to the school committee and the school committee votes on that.

97
00:09:56,103 --> 00:10:10,648
[SPEAKER_07]: And I think one of the points that this school committee has made, certainly in the time that I've been in this role and my impression is to some degree prior to that, is that part of the school committee's job and your responsibility is to advocate zealously on behalf of

98
00:10:11,264 --> 00:10:14,706
[SPEAKER_07]: the school communities that we work with and that we serve.

99
00:10:15,167 --> 00:10:24,913
[SPEAKER_07]: The municipal government, we know, and this is true for the mayor and it's true for the city council, has that responsibility, but also the responsibility to balance the competing needs of the community.

100
00:10:24,993 --> 00:10:31,778
[SPEAKER_07]: And we know that we as the school department try to be a good citizen within the constituency of municipal departments.

101
00:10:32,178 --> 00:10:39,783
[SPEAKER_07]: And so one of the points that I always make at this point in the budget season, and I know you've heard me make it this year as well, is that as the school administration

102
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:57,078
[SPEAKER_07]: We understand our responsibility to be planning for all potential contingencies, the information and the priorities that you'll hear about tonight are important priorities based on facts and based on the actual truthful needs of our students.

103
00:10:57,478 --> 00:11:02,143
[SPEAKER_07]: And at the same time we understand that we are one constituent part of the city of Medford.

104
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:06,101
[SPEAKER_07]: and we will be prepared to implement the budget that is appropriated to us.

105
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:22,828
[SPEAKER_07]: That is something you've heard me say almost verbatim each time we've talked about this, but this is an important step in that process of us making sure that we're able to articulate fully what are the needs of the school department and through that, the needs of the Medford Public Schools students.

106
00:11:23,208 --> 00:11:25,929
[SPEAKER_07]: So the superintendent's recommendation will go to the school committee.

107
00:11:25,949 --> 00:11:32,352
[SPEAKER_07]: The school committee will then make a request based on that recommendation or variation as you see fit to the municipal government.

108
00:11:32,851 --> 00:11:36,294
[SPEAKER_07]: The municipal government through the mayor and the city council will then make an appropriation.

109
00:11:36,754 --> 00:11:47,683
[SPEAKER_07]: And then that second vote that you took on June 24th last year for purposes of the FY 22 operating budget is to actually adopt the budget that is appropriated to.

110
00:11:48,123 --> 00:11:51,106
[SPEAKER_07]: You'll recall that you made a contingent vote last year.

111
00:11:51,146 --> 00:11:53,488
[SPEAKER_07]: The mayor had made a recommendation to the city council.

112
00:11:54,248 --> 00:11:56,570
[SPEAKER_07]: It represented a significant increase in our funding.

113
00:11:56,870 --> 00:12:00,513
[SPEAKER_07]: There was also about, I believe $700,000 less than that was requested.

114
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,692
[SPEAKER_07]: That was something that we were planning for.

115
00:12:04,852 --> 00:12:14,280
[SPEAKER_07]: And as you've heard me say before, that the school administration works in conjunction with the city administration to make sure, again, that we're prepared for any potential contingency.

116
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:21,126
[SPEAKER_07]: And then after that adoption, after the appropriations made by the municipal council, it is our job to implement that budget.

117
00:12:21,166 --> 00:12:28,332
[SPEAKER_07]: So you'll see the note there that there are various components of the budget process and different levers of government that have a hand in it.

118
00:12:29,599 --> 00:12:31,680
[SPEAKER_07]: It's a process that takes place.

119
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:37,641
[SPEAKER_07]: There's some variation depending on if it's a regional school committee or things of that nature, a city government versus a town government.

120
00:12:38,121 --> 00:12:45,603
[SPEAKER_07]: But generally speaking, a process like this plays out in every city and town in Massachusetts throughout, right around this time during the fiscal year.

121
00:12:46,323 --> 00:12:49,644
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay, that's it for our, sorry, one more slide.

122
00:12:49,704 --> 00:12:50,664
[SPEAKER_07]: You've seen this one before.

123
00:12:50,684 --> 00:12:56,685
[SPEAKER_07]: Again, our budget is made up in Medford of about 80% local contribution.

124
00:12:56,705 --> 00:12:59,286
[SPEAKER_07]: That's money generated by the taxpayers of Medford.

125
00:12:59,862 --> 00:13:18,890
[SPEAKER_07]: 20% or thereabouts, Chapter 70, excuse me, Chapter 70 coming in from the Commonwealth, as we talked about a little bit at your May 2nd meeting, that is based on the belief that every taxpayer in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts has a vested interest in the well-being of the students of Medford.

126
00:13:19,470 --> 00:13:24,592
[SPEAKER_07]: But the breakdown of the formula is based on the socioeconomic dynamics of the community.

127
00:13:25,153 --> 00:13:27,975
[SPEAKER_07]: or so says the Massachusetts State Legislature.

128
00:13:28,195 --> 00:13:37,019
[SPEAKER_07]: And as I said on May 2nd, I would recommend we table for the time being the degree to which we either agree or disagree with the specifics of that formula.

129
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:42,842
[SPEAKER_07]: Like many communities with a high degree of need, we would prefer not to have an 80-20 split.

130
00:13:43,663 --> 00:13:48,045
[SPEAKER_07]: But at the same time, that is what the formula essentially spits out at us right now.

131
00:13:48,445 --> 00:13:50,866
[SPEAKER_07]: And in this particular fiscal year, in FY23, we're fortunate to, as many

132
00:13:54,371 --> 00:13:58,674
[SPEAKER_07]: districts have received a significant uptick in our Chapter 70 allocation.

133
00:13:59,534 --> 00:14:12,783
[SPEAKER_07]: But again, as I reported in our labor update, given the actual breakdown in the ratio of the funding, that has not as consequential an effect as we would have preferred, perhaps, if the numbers were a little different.

134
00:14:13,264 --> 00:14:19,268
[SPEAKER_07]: You'll see that column on the far right there just representing the external funding that comes into the district.

135
00:14:19,528 --> 00:14:22,730
[SPEAKER_07]: A large portion of that is in the special education realm.

136
00:14:23,575 --> 00:14:29,597
[SPEAKER_07]: And we'll talk a little bit tonight about the fluctuation that we have to forecast for year in and year out.

137
00:14:30,577 --> 00:14:32,397
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay, now I think we can move on.

138
00:14:33,398 --> 00:14:38,939
[SPEAKER_07]: And again, this is a, I'll move quickly through this slide because it's one that you saw just a week ago.

139
00:14:39,919 --> 00:14:50,962
[SPEAKER_07]: But in the context of trying to plan for a almost or perhaps over $70 million budget, when you have nine collective bargaining agreements that represent

140
00:14:51,635 --> 00:14:56,359
[SPEAKER_07]: a very, very large portion of your budgetary expenses on a year-in and year-out basis.

141
00:14:57,220 --> 00:15:03,845
[SPEAKER_07]: You can't really sort of escape that as an important factor in your budgetary planning.

142
00:15:04,746 --> 00:15:14,434
[SPEAKER_07]: And that's something we obviously discussed at length last week and something that we'll continue to discuss as we move forward throughout this budget process.

143
00:15:14,855 --> 00:15:17,877
[SPEAKER_07]: I just want to make quick note of that third bullet point down.

144
00:15:19,151 --> 00:15:26,897
[SPEAKER_07]: where it says that a priority for the Medford Public Schools is to increase wages to maintain competitiveness while ensuring sustainability.

145
00:15:27,518 --> 00:15:33,983
[SPEAKER_07]: That is a point that you heard me articulate again in your May 2nd meeting, and I will articulate it again here.

146
00:15:34,643 --> 00:15:42,849
[SPEAKER_07]: And it is certainly the case that it is a point that we in the administration have articulated in various forums with our various bargaining partners.

147
00:15:43,470 --> 00:15:47,233
[SPEAKER_07]: We understand that we have a responsibility to maintain competitiveness.

148
00:15:48,040 --> 00:16:01,912
[SPEAKER_07]: But that responsibility is qualified by the responsibility we have to not establish financial structural deficits that would cause significant problems for the school district and the years ahead.

149
00:16:02,633 --> 00:16:11,340
[SPEAKER_07]: And so that is our job to find that equilibrium and to negotiate in good faith toward a result that achieves both ends of that objective.

150
00:16:11,998 --> 00:16:20,341
[SPEAKER_07]: And that's something that will be part of tonight's presentation, but also part of all of our efforts with respect to negotiations moving forward.

151
00:16:21,901 --> 00:16:22,922
[SPEAKER_07]: If we can go to the next one.

152
00:16:23,422 --> 00:16:26,403
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay, again, these are the priorities as summarized.

153
00:16:26,443 --> 00:16:37,027
[SPEAKER_07]: And so as we walk through the specific priorities tonight and some of the initiatives that we'll be recommending that the school district fund through one means or another,

154
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:43,241
[SPEAKER_07]: All of them, frankly, should correlate with one or multiple bullet points here.

155
00:16:44,141 --> 00:16:59,806
[SPEAKER_07]: And so, again, the reason that the negotiations are such an important component of the budgetary process is that we're an organization made up of people, and that's where most of our money goes.

156
00:16:59,886 --> 00:17:03,467
[SPEAKER_07]: So of our $67 million budget, $67.4 million budget in FY22, 54.3

157
00:17:08,613 --> 00:17:11,954
[SPEAKER_07]: approximately was money that was put into personnel expenses.

158
00:17:12,034 --> 00:17:27,977
[SPEAKER_07]: So that means that if we don't know how much the personnel is going to cost us in a given year, it can be very difficult to nail down our specific budgetary priorities that we'll be able to ultimately realize.

159
00:17:28,037 --> 00:17:35,558
[SPEAKER_07]: But as I said, it's also important for us to continue to articulate those so that the community has a full understanding as to what exactly it is

160
00:17:36,695 --> 00:17:40,277
[SPEAKER_07]: that we are looking to invest their money in, frankly.

161
00:17:40,817 --> 00:17:49,102
[SPEAKER_07]: So again, your critical priorities there, and then you'll see them in greater specificity tonight, are making sure that students have the social, emotional, and mental health supports that they need.

162
00:17:50,062 --> 00:17:55,905
[SPEAKER_07]: To the degree we're able to reduce fee-based services, that's something that this committee has identified as a critical priority.

163
00:17:56,585 --> 00:17:59,967
[SPEAKER_07]: Making sure that we're going through the responsible expansion of our CTE programming.

164
00:18:00,507 --> 00:18:04,789
[SPEAKER_07]: That is a point that we'll be discussing in greater detail in tomorrow night's budget meeting.

165
00:18:06,497 --> 00:18:22,462
