[SPEAKER_09]: A lot of those are kindergarten classrooms that when they were built didn't have projecting systems put in there, so they're using things on the cart, which is

998
01:46:25,613 --> 01:46:27,375
[SPEAKER_09]: a little tricky for the little ones.

999
01:46:27,875 --> 01:46:32,980
[SPEAKER_09]: So we looked at the best way to set up a projecting system in those classrooms.

1000
01:46:33,020 --> 01:46:41,007
[SPEAKER_09]: And at this point, with technology being the way it is, we're looking towards putting interactive panels onto the walls rather than projectors at all.

1001
01:46:41,047 --> 01:46:46,673
[SPEAKER_09]: It's a newer, more reliable, less labor-intensive installation.

1002
01:46:46,773 --> 01:46:49,936
[SPEAKER_09]: So we've had several vendors come in and give us some

1003
01:46:50,396 --> 01:46:57,778
[SPEAKER_09]: quotes for that, and it will come to about that $90,000 to put in interactive flat panel displays.

1004
01:46:57,818 --> 01:47:06,720
[SPEAKER_09]: We brought some samples in to try in both an elementary and a middle school, although almost all of these devices that are going to get installed, at least initially, will be in the K-5 schools.

1005
01:47:07,460 --> 01:47:12,821
[SPEAKER_09]: Only two in the middle schools, and actually those are both science classrooms, which just are difficult rooms to project in.

1006
01:47:12,901 --> 01:47:13,021
[SPEAKER_09]: So

1007
01:47:13,521 --> 01:47:20,109
[SPEAKER_09]: about 38 interactive panels and installed will come to about that $90,000 and those are very badly needed.

1008
01:47:20,990 --> 01:47:27,136
[SPEAKER_09]: Like I said, everyone who needs a projector has one, but it's really not an ideal setup, especially for those kindergarten classrooms.

1009
01:47:27,197 --> 01:47:30,901
[SPEAKER_09]: So that's the first wave we're hoping to be able to do for installations.

1010
01:47:33,463 --> 01:47:53,764
[SPEAKER_09]: The maker spaces, we have a maker space that actually was funded both by city funds and Verizon at the high school, which literally was delivered right before we closed the door for COVID so we are just bringing that back alive now that we can have shared spaces again and that has a lot of equipment in terms of 3D printer and

1011
01:47:55,650 --> 01:48:00,394
[SPEAKER_09]: green screen and video production and robotics and a variety of things.

1012
01:48:00,834 --> 01:48:07,900
[SPEAKER_09]: Our intent was always to put those in every library in the school, in the district, but we started with the high school.

1013
01:48:07,940 --> 01:48:12,484
[SPEAKER_09]: We're bringing that one online and the hope is that we can do it in the K-8 schools as well.

1014
01:48:13,265 --> 01:48:16,107
[SPEAKER_09]: Part of the limitation of that was our library staffing.

1015
01:48:16,788 --> 01:48:23,293
[SPEAKER_09]: And so if we can expand that library staff to be full-time certified librarians in each of those buildings, they can expand and

1016
01:48:23,993 --> 01:48:31,197
[SPEAKER_09]: and deliver a robust library curriculum, as well as support makerspace activities in each of those schools.

1017
01:48:31,257 --> 01:48:38,660
[SPEAKER_09]: So we wouldn't just put things out there that couldn't be guided use by the teachers, but we would have staff there who can actually do that.

1018
01:48:38,680 --> 01:48:42,422
[SPEAKER_09]: So it's a STEM as well as a library and literacy initiative.

1019
01:48:42,442 --> 01:48:45,003
[SPEAKER_07]: Thank you, Molly.

