[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, thank you.

1088
01:58:00,862 --> 01:58:02,682
[SPEAKER_04]: And computer, but that's another issue.

1089
01:58:02,702 --> 01:58:03,602
[SPEAKER_04]: But yes, thank you.

1090
01:58:05,043 --> 01:58:05,883
[SPEAKER_07]: Thank you for the question.

1091
01:58:06,543 --> 01:58:11,044
[SPEAKER_07]: OK, and so we sort of I can't see the hands as well either.

1092
01:58:11,064 --> 01:58:14,265
[SPEAKER_07]: So I'm going to start talking about transportation and launch in case

1093
01:58:15,310 --> 01:58:16,150
[SPEAKER_07]: there's any objection.

1094
01:58:16,930 --> 01:58:19,811
[SPEAKER_07]: And also I would say, so there's only two rows left.

1095
01:58:20,131 --> 01:58:28,233
[SPEAKER_07]: We ought to go back to the slides in a second, but just for purposes of this conversation, because I anticipate there could be questions, maybe we could just take the slide down for now.

1096
01:58:28,433 --> 01:58:30,794
[SPEAKER_07]: We're talking about these last two priorities.

1097
01:58:30,834 --> 01:58:31,694
[SPEAKER_07]: Thank you, Dr. Cushing.

1098
01:58:32,934 --> 01:58:43,217
[SPEAKER_07]: One is the transportation initiative that was implemented this year to provide free transportation for students outside of two miles through the MBTA passes.

1099
01:58:44,003 --> 01:58:47,366
[SPEAKER_07]: That program on average is costing us about $9,000 a month.

1100
01:58:47,766 --> 01:59:01,195
[SPEAKER_07]: And as I reported to you on April 11th, we think that that's, we have sufficient ESSER funding and the rationale with regard to that, I think continues into FY23.

1101
01:59:01,675 --> 01:59:07,459
[SPEAKER_07]: And so if the school committee chooses, we could continue to do that via the ESSER funding.

1102
01:59:08,620 --> 01:59:10,761
[SPEAKER_07]: It would frankly not be a significant,

1103
01:59:13,040 --> 01:59:17,083
[SPEAKER_07]: I think that's what I'll say for the time being and then take any questions.

1104
01:59:17,483 --> 01:59:32,794
[SPEAKER_07]: The lunch program initiative is frankly of a much different story because we at the moment are looking to get it ascertained with greater specificity where we are with regard to our revolving fund in our school lunch program.

1105
01:59:33,355 --> 01:59:40,440
[SPEAKER_07]: We're confident that we will have a surplus, but given that under the USDA regulations,

1106
01:59:40,980 --> 01:59:53,947
[SPEAKER_07]: all school lunches free right now, we are not confident that we can ascertain with certainty or with enough certainty, I should say, what the participation rate will be.

1107
01:59:54,567 --> 02:00:04,352
[SPEAKER_07]: And in the absence of that variable, it is difficult for us to project exactly what the potential cost would be of going to free lunch district wide.

1108
02:00:04,812 --> 02:00:08,494
[SPEAKER_07]: And so at this time, those numbers are too uncertain.

1109
02:00:08,674 --> 02:00:09,355
[SPEAKER_07]: The administration,

1110
02:00:09,887 --> 02:00:14,351
[SPEAKER_07]: can't recommend that we move forward with a free lunch across the district.

1111
02:00:14,791 --> 02:00:23,939
[SPEAKER_07]: We fully understand that this is a priority of the committee, and we have a responsibility to try to identify other ways in which we may be able to move in that direction.

1112
02:00:24,299 --> 02:00:33,967
[SPEAKER_07]: But at the moment, we're monitoring and hopeful about the legislation that's making its way through Beacon Hill, which would, frankly, absolve us of this problem.

1113
02:00:35,422 --> 02:00:47,324
[SPEAKER_07]: and certainly something that the district supports fully, but the implementation of that at the district level, I do think has a significant amount of financial uncertainty.

1114
02:00:47,364 --> 02:00:52,965
[SPEAKER_07]: And so we would be hesitant to recommend its implementation for next year, absent that legislation.

1115
02:00:58,426 --> 02:00:58,706
[SPEAKER_06]: Mayor?

1116
02:01:02,107 --> 02:01:02,827
[SPEAKER_06]: Member Rousseau.

1117
02:01:04,907 --> 02:01:05,287
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

1118
02:01:06,308 --> 02:01:12,450
[SPEAKER_13]: I would say that implementing this is actually, it's current policy of the school committee that it will be included and implemented.

1119
02:01:13,230 --> 02:01:32,957
[SPEAKER_13]: So I understand that the recommendation is that we are not going to assume that we can afford this, but I would say that absent a school committee member, any one of the seven of us putting something on the agenda to reverse a decision that we have already made, I don't really see it as a optional program at this point.

1120
02:01:34,211 --> 02:01:39,515
[SPEAKER_13]: Unless I have the wording of the motion that we passed seven zero, which I'm happy to read.

1121
02:01:40,295 --> 02:01:41,676
[SPEAKER_13]: If anybody wants to see it.

1122
02:01:42,157 --> 02:01:45,859
[SPEAKER_13]: But at the moment, it's the policy of the school committee that it will be free.

1123
02:01:47,761 --> 02:01:54,926
[SPEAKER_13]: So I just that's just a reminder because it didn't sound like it was being treated as our policy when in fact it is.

1124
02:01:56,007 --> 02:02:00,971
[SPEAKER_07]: So I want to say that I appreciate fully the

1125
02:02:02,506 --> 02:02:05,188
[SPEAKER_07]: the intent of the policy that was adopted.

1126
02:02:05,969 --> 02:02:13,615
[SPEAKER_07]: And I think it's from a principal perspective, it's one that the administration fully agrees with.

1127
02:02:15,436 --> 02:02:20,400
[SPEAKER_07]: I do not recall being consulted prior to the passing of that policy.

1128
02:02:20,420 --> 02:02:26,464
[SPEAKER_07]: It was something that was introduced on the floor and I believe I was asked to speak to extemporaneously.

1129
02:02:26,484 --> 02:02:31,248
[SPEAKER_07]: And the council that the committee was provided at the time

1130
02:02:31,833 --> 02:02:41,758
[SPEAKER_07]: was that for FY22, the implications of it were essentially non-existent because it was something that was being afforded to all school districts across the country.

1131
02:02:42,398 --> 02:02:48,421
[SPEAKER_07]: But if the committee wants us to move forward with this, we can continue to explore those options.

1132
02:02:48,541 --> 02:02:57,346
[SPEAKER_07]: But I have a responsibility to report to the committee that it has an uncertain financial implication.

1133
02:02:57,926 --> 02:03:00,487
[SPEAKER_07]: And given all of the other uncertainty,

1134
02:03:03,163 --> 02:03:10,486
[SPEAKER_07]: in this budget that we're trying to develop, I think it takes on a degree of risk that would be imprudent.

1135
02:03:11,086 --> 02:03:14,008
[SPEAKER_07]: At the end, as you point out, it is up to the committee.

1136
02:03:17,969 --> 02:03:18,589
[SPEAKER_05]: Member Russo?

1137
02:03:19,270 --> 02:03:19,590
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

1138
02:03:19,910 --> 02:03:23,351
[SPEAKER_13]: I don't, for a second, want to discount what we're asking you to do.

1139
02:03:26,173 --> 02:03:30,134
[SPEAKER_13]: We had a pretty heated discussion during the conversations around the discipline policy.

1140
02:03:32,688 --> 02:03:37,192
[SPEAKER_13]: even hinted that you all would just do what was right even if it was violating the policy.

1141
02:03:37,872 --> 02:03:46,519
[SPEAKER_13]: There were at least two of the senior administrative staff who were very unhappy with the notion that we would pass a policy where you could just violate it.

1142
02:03:47,700 --> 02:03:52,824
[SPEAKER_13]: And I think that either we're gonna follow policy or not.

1143
02:03:53,545 --> 02:03:57,288
[SPEAKER_13]: And it can't just be when it's you don't like that sentence

1144
02:03:57,939 --> 02:04:04,988
[SPEAKER_13]: And I certainly respected and apologize for suggesting that we should just write a policy that you'll just violate when you feel the need to.

1145
02:04:05,789 --> 02:04:15,422
[SPEAKER_13]: And I am completely on board with the notion that our policies are not, you know, if we leave wiggle room, wiggle room, we put the wiggle room in the language.

1146
02:04:17,054 --> 02:04:19,696
[SPEAKER_13]: And that's what we did for the discipline policy actually.

1147
02:04:20,597 --> 02:04:26,041
[SPEAKER_13]: But I just want to be clear that the policy we passed did not include wiggle room.

1148
02:04:26,421 --> 02:04:27,262
[SPEAKER_13]: There are seven members.

1149
02:04:27,763 --> 02:04:38,091
[SPEAKER_13]: If any one of us wants to write a motion to rescind that, or it wouldn't be rescind at this point, but to make a motion to start charging kids for food, that would have to be on a regular agenda.

1150
02:04:38,211 --> 02:04:42,594
[SPEAKER_13]: And until that happens, I don't really think we're having a conversation about whether it will be in the budget.

1151
02:04:44,055 --> 02:04:46,057
[SPEAKER_13]: That's just my opinion based on

1152
02:04:46,813 --> 02:04:53,657
[SPEAKER_13]: the vote of seven members who in affirmative said we will no longer charge for food.

1153
02:04:54,237 --> 02:04:54,477
[SPEAKER_07]: Right.

1154
02:04:54,637 --> 02:05:05,403
[SPEAKER_07]: But I think, and I appreciate that, but we're not charging for lunch right now based on the federal regulations and on the committee's directive, right?

1155
02:05:06,764 --> 02:05:08,165
[SPEAKER_07]: We haven't charged anyone for lunch.

1156
02:05:08,745 --> 02:05:15,629
[SPEAKER_07]: What I'm saying is that if you don't want us to charge anyone for lunch next year, we're going to need to

1157
02:05:16,672 --> 02:05:27,519
[SPEAKER_07]: factor that into the budget, because there is going to be a cost associated with that, unless, as I think we all hope, the legislature takes that out of our hands.

1158
02:05:28,159 --> 02:05:44,650
[SPEAKER_07]: At the moment, where the reason that I would be inclined to recommend that the directive be suspended, at least for the upcoming school year, is that I can't even tell you what that cost would be, because there's really no way of making a projection

1159
02:05:45,238 --> 02:05:50,679
[SPEAKER_07]: with respect to participation, which is what would, in part, determine the financial implication.

1160
02:05:51,039 --> 02:06:04,523
[SPEAKER_07]: So I think it is necessary to talk about in the budget, because if the committee feels as though we should continue to move forward with the mandate, then we're going to have to take a guess on the financial implication.

1161
02:06:05,623 --> 02:06:09,544
[SPEAKER_07]: It may cause a significant number of other disruptions.

1162
02:06:10,284 --> 02:06:15,005
[SPEAKER_07]: And I can't tell you exactly what those are, because I can't tell you exactly what the financial implication of it is.

1163
02:06:15,478 --> 02:06:19,499
[SPEAKER_07]: So that is why it's coming up in the context of the budget.

1164
02:06:21,720 --> 02:06:22,800
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Mr. Murphy.

1165
02:06:22,840 --> 02:06:24,101
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Mr. Russo.

1166
02:06:24,121 --> 02:06:29,342
[SPEAKER_05]: And if I just may speak on that issue, I too want everybody to have free lunch.

1167
02:06:29,422 --> 02:06:34,944
[SPEAKER_05]: And I pray that the state comes through for us because it could be upwards of a million and a half dollars.

1168
02:06:35,004 --> 02:06:38,085
[SPEAKER_05]: So I just want to make sure that that's clear.

1169
02:06:38,145 --> 02:06:43,827
[SPEAKER_05]: And if it's between children that can afford it paying and teachers or

1170
02:06:45,580 --> 02:06:50,263
[SPEAKER_05]: Professionals not having a job, we just, we have to weigh those options as we move forward.

1171
02:06:51,184 --> 02:06:56,927
[SPEAKER_05]: Member Hayes.

1172
02:06:57,668 --> 02:07:05,333
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought, my understanding was that it was currently in the budget, the state budget to fund lunches through next year.

1173
02:07:07,264 --> 02:07:09,304
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know if anyone else has more information on that.

1174
02:07:09,324 --> 02:07:15,526
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought, I thought that was kind of a done deal already, but it just, the budget isn't a done deal.

1175
02:07:15,566 --> 02:07:19,327
[SPEAKER_07]: So yes, my understanding is that there's support at the legislature.

1176
02:07:19,347 --> 02:07:25,428
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't, I'm hopeful, but this is something that would have a significant impact on our district.

1177
02:07:25,448 --> 02:07:36,991
[SPEAKER_07]: So it's sort of one of those ones that we can't really rely on them because if we plan for it, if we plan to implement this directive and

1178
02:07:38,463 --> 02:07:41,846
[SPEAKER_07]: you know, we don't have a potential cost associated with it.

1179
02:07:42,346 --> 02:07:57,899
[SPEAKER_07]: And the legislature, for whatever reason, does not, if it doesn't make it into the final version of the budget, the state budget, we could have created a significant problem for ourselves locally, which is again, why I think we have a responsibility to discuss it here.

1180
02:08:03,023 --> 02:08:05,325
[SPEAKER_04]: May I, Mayor?

1181
02:08:05,385 --> 02:08:06,426
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, Member McLaughlin.

1182
02:08:07,237 --> 02:08:12,799
[SPEAKER_04]: And, but we won't know that right until July one, whether it's, it's been passed in the governor's budget.

1183
02:08:13,639 --> 02:08:14,019
[SPEAKER_07]: Correct.

1184
02:08:14,400 --> 02:08:16,980
[SPEAKER_07]: I mean, presumably, yes.

1185
02:08:17,041 --> 02:08:20,042
[SPEAKER_07]: I mean, there's been times where I don't think it'll be before July one.

1186
02:08:20,102 --> 02:08:23,443
[SPEAKER_07]: And there are times where certain provisions have ample support.

1187
02:08:23,463 --> 02:08:35,387
[SPEAKER_07]: So you can start to rely on it in times when, you know, there are continuing resolutions of some kind and it goes further into the summer, but that sort of, I think more that sort of

1188
02:08:36,148 --> 02:08:40,312
[SPEAKER_06]: reaffirms the point that there's a lot of uncertainty associated with this.

1189
02:08:44,295 --> 02:08:44,675
[SPEAKER_06]: Mayor?

1190
02:08:45,736 --> 02:08:50,100
[SPEAKER_05]: Member Graham, Member Russo.

1191
02:08:51,761 --> 02:08:58,287
[SPEAKER_02]: If I recall back, maybe even like before the pandemic, when we were talking about

1192
02:09:00,847 --> 02:09:01,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Um, a pilot.

1193
02:09:02,048 --> 02:09:08,310
[SPEAKER_02]: We did a pilot way back when and a big central part of that was like tracking participation.

1194
02:09:08,330 --> 02:09:19,014
[SPEAKER_02]: Um, to help inform like whether we would qualify for some program or or another, um, After that, and it was this like predated Mr Murphy for sure.

1195
02:09:19,034 --> 02:09:20,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Um Are.

1196
02:09:22,035 --> 02:09:25,096
[SPEAKER_02]: I think I heard you say you can't size this.

1197
02:09:25,236 --> 02:09:29,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Does that is that because we're not tracking or because we lack data?

1198
02:09:30,752 --> 02:09:32,933
[SPEAKER_02]: from the city in the revolving account?

1199
02:09:32,973 --> 02:09:34,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, why can't we size this?

1200
02:09:35,433 --> 02:09:36,734
[SPEAKER_07]: We can track participation.

1201
02:09:36,754 --> 02:09:43,575
[SPEAKER_07]: The concern is that with all of lunch being free this year, that participation could be at one number.

1202
02:09:44,116 --> 02:09:47,697
[SPEAKER_07]: And if we were to charge, the participation would be another number.

1203
02:09:48,317 --> 02:09:59,220
[SPEAKER_07]: Or if it's free in a second year, I mean, all of those variables could have an impact on what the potential reimbursement would be.

1204
02:10:01,505 --> 02:10:05,606
[SPEAKER_07]: And so that, again, if the committee wants us to attempt to do this, we can.

1205
02:10:05,666 --> 02:10:11,288
[SPEAKER_07]: But what would happen is we will have to come back with a number, with a projection.

1206
02:10:11,308 --> 02:10:19,510
[SPEAKER_07]: I will be inclined to be conservative with that projection because of the potential domino effect that would occur if we miss.

1207
02:10:20,411 --> 02:10:25,512
[SPEAKER_07]: And then we can put it to the committee for consideration.

1208
02:10:29,898 --> 02:10:31,980
[SPEAKER_07]: there's a significant amount of risk involved with this.

1209
02:10:33,361 --> 02:10:51,173
[SPEAKER_07]: Last year, the food services budget finished with an operating deficit of over a half million dollars, which we were able to pull back to some degree with some funding that we received subsequent based on appropriations related to specifically for food services in different school districts.

1210
02:10:51,273 --> 02:10:55,536
[SPEAKER_07]: But the deficit was significant.

1211
02:10:55,896 --> 02:10:57,337
[SPEAKER_07]: We expect to surplus this year

1212
02:10:58,001 --> 02:11:05,405
[SPEAKER_07]: Um, but again, to the point you just raised, we don't have, we don't have those numbers, um, to the penny at the moment.

1213
02:11:07,506 --> 02:11:20,952
[SPEAKER_02]: And, um, I think also before your time led by the mayor, the entire city signed a hunger free pledge and advocates from the

1214
02:11:22,488 --> 02:11:42,283
[SPEAKER_02]: task force, I don't know exactly like the right term to use for the people actually trying to make sure that we politicians keep good to our commitment to make Medford hunger free have reached out and asked us asked several of us about, you know, what, you know,

1215
02:11:44,050 --> 02:11:47,531
[SPEAKER_02]: what Medford Public Schools, you know, how Medford Public Schools fits into all of this.

1216
02:11:49,412 --> 02:11:59,536
[SPEAKER_02]: And what I have relayed to them is that I don't think there's anyone on the committee who is not interested in proceeding as we have stated in our policy.

1217
02:11:59,716 --> 02:12:00,096
[SPEAKER_02]: However,

1218
02:12:02,597 --> 02:12:10,240
[SPEAKER_02]: you know, that is all contingent on funding that comes from the mayor and the city in terms of how we spend.

1219
02:12:10,621 --> 02:12:26,748
[SPEAKER_02]: And in lean times, we start to have to pit things like feeding kids lunch against administrators and that that's like never a good situation to be in because everybody loses when the community has to have that

1220
02:12:27,909 --> 02:12:36,093
[SPEAKER_02]: kind of debate about money as it relates to basic necessity and needs.

1221
02:12:36,173 --> 02:12:51,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So I say all that to say there are people who probably are or may not be on this call who are watching at the city level to make sure that the city keeps good on its promises that were unanimous.

1222
02:12:53,400 --> 02:12:55,341
[SPEAKER_02]: And Mayor, I don't know if they've reached out to you.

1223
02:12:55,561 --> 02:12:57,142
[SPEAKER_02]: I know there's

1224
02:12:57,677 --> 02:13:04,738
[SPEAKER_02]: or it sounds like there is actual activity happening on that task force that the school committee has not been made aware of, which I think is great.

1225
02:13:04,879 --> 02:13:11,280
[SPEAKER_02]: I also think the school committee needs to like be looped in somehow in a formal way, which I think was the intent.

1226
02:13:12,260 --> 02:13:25,163
[SPEAKER_02]: But overall, I think as we proceed, I think we do need to at least understand what it would cost to do this thing that we've all promised to do really rather excitedly and unanimously.

1227
02:13:27,660 --> 02:13:30,041
[SPEAKER_02]: at multiple times actually over the last couple of years.

1228
02:13:30,101 --> 02:13:40,504
[SPEAKER_02]: So I also think based on what I am hearing that the outlook is good and favorable from a statewide perspective.

1229
02:13:40,604 --> 02:13:53,869
[SPEAKER_02]: So I am of the mind that like, I want us to be ready with an understanding of the impact in case that goes South, but that I'm actually not overly worried that it will go South.

1230
02:13:53,989 --> 02:13:56,710
[SPEAKER_02]: I just want us to be prepared to be able to decide

1231
02:13:58,146 --> 02:14:02,266
[SPEAKER_02]: what the implications are for us if it does.

1232
02:14:03,449 --> 02:14:08,471
[SPEAKER_02]: But I know that's a lot of work and a bunch of information you may or may not have.

1233
02:14:08,531 --> 02:14:10,992
[SPEAKER_02]: So I don't know how best to proceed with that.

1234
02:14:11,212 --> 02:14:13,493
[SPEAKER_07]: Just to be clear, it's more the latter than the amount of work.

1235
02:14:13,513 --> 02:14:15,814
[SPEAKER_07]: The amount of the work, I think, is Ms.

1236
02:14:15,854 --> 02:14:18,055
[SPEAKER_07]: Graham's point where the committee feels unanimously about this.

1237
02:14:18,075 --> 02:14:22,157
[SPEAKER_07]: There's no one in the school administration that really wants to charge students for lunch either.

1238
02:14:22,197 --> 02:14:27,699
[SPEAKER_07]: I think, again, the philosophical underpinnings of this are something that we all agree with.

1239
02:14:27,779 --> 02:14:33,101
[SPEAKER_07]: And we've talked in the past about not wanting to have a public school system that's

1240
02:14:33,857 --> 02:14:37,580
[SPEAKER_07]: littered with with user generated fees to fund it.

1241
02:14:37,620 --> 02:14:41,103
[SPEAKER_07]: So I think there's unanimity of use on the preference here.

1242
02:14:41,923 --> 02:14:55,533
[SPEAKER_07]: But from a practical perspective, and I think just using the point that Miss Graham makes about the city, you know, when we make a budget request that's based on a number of FTEs, we can multiply those FTEs by this year 70,000.

1243
02:14:56,134 --> 02:15:00,357
[SPEAKER_07]: And if it turns out that some of the personnel we retain for those FTEs

1244
02:15:00,830 --> 02:15:12,836
[SPEAKER_07]: costs a little bit more or a little bit less based on the experience of the individuals coming in, there's a certain level of, again, art, not science, to those projections.

1245
02:15:12,896 --> 02:15:19,800
[SPEAKER_07]: And we can feel as though we're doing right by the committee and right by the community, as long as we're able to stay within that reasonable range.

1246
02:15:20,400 --> 02:15:29,945
[SPEAKER_07]: In a situation like this, in which we frankly, we can come up with a number, but I have a responsibility to tell the committee that it's not really based in anything

1247
02:15:31,113 --> 02:15:46,861
[SPEAKER_07]: particularly factual, and I have concerns that the number could turn out to be way off, then either the city could appropriate significant more funding that is necessary to advance this particular priority, or it could appropriate funding that could be very much short of that priority.

1248
02:15:47,262 --> 02:15:59,328
[SPEAKER_07]: And in that world, when we're advocating before the municipal government, the mayor and the council, I am hesitant to just not be able to recommendation that as a school administration, we can stand behind

1249
02:16:00,099 --> 02:16:06,144
[SPEAKER_07]: with a certain degree of confidence, and that's why I feel the need to flag this particular initiative.

1250
02:16:07,746 --> 02:16:13,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and I understand and appreciate that.

1251
02:16:14,111 --> 02:16:25,942
[SPEAKER_02]: I do think the other thing this committee needs perhaps more education on, whether from the Food Security Task Force or somebody else, is

1252
02:16:27,385 --> 02:16:35,853
[SPEAKER_02]: The value of providing free lunch has less to do with feeding a kid whose parents may be able to afford it.

1253
02:16:35,873 --> 02:16:37,635
[SPEAKER_02]: It is not about that at all.

1254
02:16:38,436 --> 02:16:45,943
[SPEAKER_02]: It is about opening up access to the kids who desperately need to be able to eat lunch at school.

1255
02:16:46,735 --> 02:16:57,883
[SPEAKER_02]: For for a whole variety of reasons, who may or may not qualify under the states program whose parents may or may not want to qualify them under the free lunch programs and.

1256
02:16:59,464 --> 02:17:01,766
[SPEAKER_02]: When it becomes a normalized part of the process.

1257
02:17:02,977 --> 02:17:03,758
[SPEAKER_02]: everybody wins.

1258
02:17:04,178 --> 02:17:14,024
[SPEAKER_02]: And when every kid in school isn't hungry, every kid in school benefits from all the kids in school being able to focus on learning and not worrying about being hungry.

1259
02:17:14,104 --> 02:17:17,566
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'm like far from the expert in the room on this topic.

1260
02:17:17,746 --> 02:17:19,967
[SPEAKER_02]: I know there are people who feel way more passionately than I do.

1261
02:17:19,988 --> 02:17:31,695
[SPEAKER_02]: I think my suggestion for the superintendent and I can follow up with you in an email is that we find somebody from the food security task force to engage with us to make sure we're all on the same page about

1262
02:17:33,195 --> 02:17:41,001
[SPEAKER_02]: this program and how it fits into the master plan for the excited declaration that we made about being hunger free here in Medford.

1263
02:17:46,404 --> 02:17:49,907
[SPEAKER_07]: We can definitely follow up with that and provide that information.

1264
02:17:49,947 --> 02:18:00,754
[SPEAKER_07]: I just I just I think I wanted from the outset as I did last month, just flag this as something that while there are some things that if if

1265
02:18:01,872 --> 02:18:04,894
[SPEAKER_07]: If our request is fulfilled, we know what we can do and how.

1266
02:18:05,355 --> 02:18:07,476
[SPEAKER_07]: In this case, I don't even know what the request would be.

1267
02:18:07,516 --> 02:18:09,197
[SPEAKER_07]: So that's just something we'll have to work on.

1268
02:18:09,217 --> 02:18:11,559
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you.

1269
02:18:11,579 --> 02:18:12,200
[SPEAKER_06]: Member Rousseau?

1270
02:18:15,722 --> 02:18:16,263
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you, Mayor.

1271
02:18:17,063 --> 02:18:19,485
[SPEAKER_13]: I just want to call out a couple of things.

1272
02:18:19,665 --> 02:18:26,810
[SPEAKER_13]: First of all, I understand that there is literally no one, probably no one even in the entire Zoom here, who wants to charge kids for food.

1273
02:18:28,612 --> 02:18:29,432
[SPEAKER_13]: Maybe I'm wrong, but

1274
02:18:30,975 --> 02:18:38,162
[SPEAKER_13]: You know, the drop in participation is absolutely will happen.

1275
02:18:38,522 --> 02:18:45,448
[SPEAKER_13]: As we know this last year, participation was very robust over previous normal years.

1276
02:18:47,070 --> 02:18:50,012
[SPEAKER_13]: And, you know, the effects of the pandemic are not done.

1277
02:18:50,192 --> 02:18:51,914
[SPEAKER_13]: There are lots of people who are still hurting.

1278
02:18:52,675 --> 02:18:55,858
[SPEAKER_13]: And as Member Graham mentioned, there are lots of people for which applying

1279
02:18:58,214 --> 02:19:01,296
[SPEAKER_13]: represents in their minds a security risk to their families.

1280
02:19:01,797 --> 02:19:05,359
[SPEAKER_13]: And it's not really an appropriate decision that anybody should have to make.

1281
02:19:05,759 --> 02:19:08,421
[SPEAKER_13]: And I don't think anybody here is saying that's okay either.

1282
02:19:09,882 --> 02:19:12,704
[SPEAKER_13]: I do have a question about whether or not ESSER funds could be used for this.

1283
02:19:12,724 --> 02:19:17,308
[SPEAKER_07]: I think that there's an argument that they could.

1284
02:19:17,328 --> 02:19:27,295
[SPEAKER_07]: And I think that's one of the, I mean, that might be something we have to look at, but this is one with an unknown price tag.

1285
02:19:27,974 --> 02:19:34,500
[SPEAKER_07]: And depending on what that price tag is, it could have a disproportionate impact on the availability of the ESSER funding.

1286
02:19:35,060 --> 02:19:39,504
[SPEAKER_07]: Not in a way that I think somehow this could blow through, you know, multi-million dollars.

1287
02:19:39,524 --> 02:19:41,265
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't think that would happen.

1288
02:19:42,847 --> 02:19:51,153
[SPEAKER_07]: But what could happen is that essentially we pump this issue down the road a year or two.

1289
02:19:51,954 --> 02:19:54,917
[SPEAKER_07]: And then as the ESSER funding is expiring, particularly if the

1290
02:19:55,821 --> 02:20:06,069
[SPEAKER_07]: and the appetite of the Legislature has diminished for this then we could just move this problem further down the road.

1291
02:20:06,910 --> 02:20:17,358
[SPEAKER_07]: And I would say just sort of like thinking about this from a political science perspective, if every district were to do that, the incentive for the Legislature to do something about it would probably be diminished as well.

1292
02:20:19,179 --> 02:20:24,944
[SPEAKER_07]: And so I don't, again, I do think that, again, I don't want to

1293
02:20:26,194 --> 02:20:34,461
[SPEAKER_07]: interpret the ESSER eligibility in a way that is so liberal that it diminishes some of the other ways in which we're interpreting it.

1294
02:20:35,943 --> 02:20:46,272
[SPEAKER_07]: But my sort of gut reaction having reviewed ESSER 2 and ESSER 3 guidelines is yes, I do think we could probably construe them to include this.

1295
02:20:46,712 --> 02:20:51,256
[SPEAKER_07]: I just don't know if that would necessarily be a strategic or prudent thing to do.

1296
02:20:54,004 --> 02:20:54,884
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

1297
02:20:54,904 --> 02:20:58,185
[SPEAKER_13]: I have been in contact with the Medford Food Security Task Force.

1298
02:20:58,805 --> 02:21:10,288
[SPEAKER_13]: They were reaching out about how we can, on the school committee, continue to move us towards the Hunger-Free Medford Proclamation that was made.

1299
02:21:11,188 --> 02:21:16,949
[SPEAKER_13]: And I had indicated that it is the current policy of the school committee that this will continue.

1300
02:21:18,629 --> 02:21:20,430
[SPEAKER_13]: I didn't answer that as a school committee

1301
02:21:22,083 --> 02:21:25,685
[SPEAKER_13]: as our opinion, but in fact, it was already voted so on.

1302
02:21:26,466 --> 02:21:46,517
[SPEAKER_13]: And, you know, I think that the proclamation to make Medford hunger free is, is, well, the city council made it, I don't actually remember if the mayor's office at the time was a participant in that, but, you know, it's, it's three years old, two years old, the proclamation, three years old, I think.

1303
02:21:46,797 --> 02:21:46,917
[SPEAKER_13]: And,

1304
02:21:48,679 --> 02:21:53,683
[SPEAKER_13]: you know, that's not a lot of time to be deciding we're just gonna give up on proclamations that we made a big deal about.

1305
02:21:53,883 --> 02:22:02,869
[SPEAKER_13]: So not having this funded and free is moving us further away from making Medford hunger free.

1306
02:22:02,909 --> 02:22:05,030
[SPEAKER_13]: I think that should be universally understood.

1307
02:22:05,191 --> 02:22:15,718
[SPEAKER_13]: So if one direction is that there's nobody hungry in Medford, and one is that there are more people hungry in Medford, not including this in the budget will mean we have decided

1308
02:22:17,848 --> 02:22:26,656
[SPEAKER_13]: not the administrators, this is not a moral conversation about your desires and intent, but we will have decided to make Medford more hungry.

1309
02:22:28,237 --> 02:22:31,040
[SPEAKER_13]: It's just a simple fact, can't be denied.

1310
02:22:31,200 --> 02:22:32,081
[SPEAKER_13]: So thank you.

1311
02:22:34,343 --> 02:22:35,544
[SPEAKER_07]: We'll report back on this.

1312
02:22:35,804 --> 02:22:38,787
[SPEAKER_07]: I think the committee has heard our concerns.

1313
02:22:39,427 --> 02:22:40,268
[SPEAKER_07]: We'll try to

1314
02:22:42,351 --> 02:22:45,372
[SPEAKER_07]: address this with as much specificity as we can.

1315
02:22:45,452 --> 02:22:57,914
[SPEAKER_07]: Just understand that I do think there's a little, there's a degree of speculation here that I'm not comfortable with that may turn out to be unavoidable, but we'll present you with that information in the coming weeks.

1316
02:23:00,935 --> 02:23:10,157
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay, I think we're ready for the final two slides that sort of sum up where we've landed with regard to these priorities.

1317
02:23:10,217 --> 02:23:10,697
[SPEAKER_07]: And I guess

1318
02:23:11,348 --> 02:23:24,798
[SPEAKER_07]: As Dr. Cushing prepares slide 16 for us, I will say that just like the categories can potentially shift based on feedback from the committee and even some of the comments that were made tonight were very helpful, we appreciate them.

1319
02:23:26,259 --> 02:23:38,848
[SPEAKER_07]: As other funding sources are identified, or as we frankly just deliberate upon some of these priorities, between now and the time that the school committee adopts the budget,

1320
02:23:39,678 --> 02:23:45,542
[SPEAKER_07]: We'll continue to communicate that clearly and make sure that the committee is aware of some of those fluctuations.

1321
02:23:45,922 --> 02:24:01,373
[SPEAKER_07]: You remember last year that their committee requested one number than the municipal government maybe appropriation, and there was a $760,000 Delta that needed to be bridged and so we identified some of the pieces, and I think it might have been the math coaches last year.

1322
02:24:01,826 --> 02:24:09,531
[SPEAKER_07]: that we sort of had as a priority that had been listed throughout, but we had said that that might be something that there could be some wiggle room on.

1323
02:24:09,771 --> 02:24:14,254
[SPEAKER_07]: Frankly, that's another one this year that there could be some wiggle room on.

1324
02:24:14,454 --> 02:24:23,339
[SPEAKER_07]: And so the $71.4 million number represented, and another caveat that I always mention, and I will again, these are all approximations.

1325
02:24:23,499 --> 02:24:28,022
[SPEAKER_07]: So when you finally, when you get your budget book, if where we land is $71.4 million,

1326
02:24:29,605 --> 02:24:35,448
[SPEAKER_07]: it'll be $71 million, $430,000, and a few other numbers and some change.

1327
02:24:35,488 --> 02:24:48,073
[SPEAKER_07]: But for purposes of this discussion, if we were to incorporate category A only into the operating budget, we would be looking at a $71.4 million request.

1328
02:24:48,713 --> 02:24:52,215
[SPEAKER_07]: And the way we reach that number moving left to right is the 67.4.

1329
02:24:52,315 --> 02:24:53,135
[SPEAKER_07]: That includes the 2% in FY

1330
02:24:58,307 --> 02:25:06,534
[SPEAKER_07]: 22 that has not yet been implemented and is still being negotiated, but has been identified throughout our budgeting process going back to last spring.

1331
02:25:06,554 --> 02:25:11,178
[SPEAKER_07]: Then there's the fixed costs that bring us up to 69.9 million.

1332
02:25:11,218 --> 02:25:17,343
[SPEAKER_07]: That's the $2.5 million costs in contractual wage increases that are due in FY 23.

1333
02:25:18,224 --> 02:25:23,509
[SPEAKER_07]: That's your special education tuition and specialized transportation costs.

1334
02:25:24,009 --> 02:25:25,130
[SPEAKER_07]: And then a few other variable

1335
02:25:25,740 --> 02:25:28,802
[SPEAKER_07]: fixed cost increases that we're projecting in the hundreds of thousands.

1336
02:25:30,122 --> 02:25:37,686
[SPEAKER_07]: If we were to incorporate a 2% FY23 cost of living adjustment, that would bring that number up to 71 million.

1337
02:25:38,387 --> 02:25:43,609
[SPEAKER_07]: If we were to incorporate all of the category A and category B, that number would rise to 72 million.

1338
02:25:43,929 --> 02:25:52,354
[SPEAKER_07]: But understanding that all of category B are items that we're prepared to support through the ESSER funding, that brings that number back down to 71.4.

1339
02:25:54,827 --> 02:26:01,451
[SPEAKER_07]: So we can go to the next one, I think, just in terms of some final points on slide 17.

1340
02:26:04,192 --> 02:26:08,975
[SPEAKER_07]: Again, I said this a lot last year, but we had a good conversation and appreciate Ms.

1341
02:26:09,015 --> 02:26:15,839
[SPEAKER_07]: Bowen's participation tonight with regard to our special education costs, but those numbers in particular do fluctuate.

1342
02:26:15,899 --> 02:26:24,304
[SPEAKER_07]: And so we're keeping a close eye on that, understanding that we'll see some budgetary relief in August, usually,

1343
02:26:24,818 --> 02:26:30,561
[SPEAKER_07]: September the latest with regard to our circuit breaker and IDEA funding.

1344
02:26:31,241 --> 02:26:32,962
[SPEAKER_07]: So there'll be some offsets there.

1345
02:26:33,803 --> 02:26:39,085
[SPEAKER_07]: Our responsibility is to continue to be looking for efficiencies and savings as we can identify them.

1346
02:26:39,105 --> 02:26:44,588
[SPEAKER_07]: We talked a lot about the strategic considerations related to the operating budget and ESSER priorities.

1347
02:26:45,008 --> 02:26:50,031
[SPEAKER_07]: And again, that's about, is this one time, is this recurring, and also is this urgent?

1348
02:26:51,035 --> 02:27:00,318
[SPEAKER_07]: and something that we need to implement in order to address student need and what is the nexus between that need and the pandemic and the disruptions related to the pandemic.

1349
02:27:01,178 --> 02:27:04,639
[SPEAKER_07]: Negotiations, again, that's our biggest budgetary driver.

1350
02:27:04,979 --> 02:27:10,200
[SPEAKER_07]: That's why we've accounted for those to a large degree in our FY23 projections here.

1351
02:27:10,800 --> 02:27:17,262
[SPEAKER_07]: And then regardless of what happens, we will continue to plan for any potential contingencies

1352
02:27:18,593 --> 02:27:38,724
[SPEAKER_07]: that may arise and ultimately we understand that we're one component of the city of Medford and whatever that appropriation is, we are going to implement the FY23 budget in a way that prioritizes student needs and recovering from the disruption that we know all of our school communities have felt over the past couple of years.

1353
02:27:38,744 --> 02:27:45,528
[SPEAKER_07]: So tomorrow we'll talk a little bit more about some of our staffing issues and vocational school

1354
02:27:46,405 --> 02:27:54,167
[SPEAKER_07]: some of the challenges that are facing our specific school communities, and we look forward to speaking with you.

1355
02:27:55,168 --> 02:28:07,091
[SPEAKER_07]: Then, I don't, just from a timing housekeeping standpoint, we ran a little over our projected time tonight, although I think the calendar invite suggested that the time was any longer, which I apologize for.

1356
02:28:07,471 --> 02:28:08,491
[SPEAKER_07]: The goal was two hours.

1357
02:28:10,272 --> 02:28:13,393
[SPEAKER_07]: Tomorrow night, I wouldn't anticipate that being the case, but I do think

1358
02:28:14,414 --> 02:28:16,816
[SPEAKER_07]: that will need to pick this up again at your regular meeting on May 16.

1359
02:28:18,097 --> 02:28:26,703
[SPEAKER_07]: And we'll be looking for feedback in terms of other issues or priorities that you think would be helpful to have a public platform to flesh out more fully.

1360
02:28:27,704 --> 02:28:30,526
[SPEAKER_07]: So if there are any final questions, I'm happy to take them, but I think we're good on the slides.

1361
02:28:31,326 --> 02:28:33,228
[SPEAKER_12]: Member Russo?

1362
02:28:35,147 --> 02:28:35,827
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

